WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.251 --> 00:00:08.505
When you think of a war reporter,
who do you picture?

00:00:10.924 --> 00:00:12.175
Someone like this?

00:00:15.178 --> 00:00:16.596
Or someone like this?

00:00:18.306 --> 00:00:19.558
Maybe him?

00:00:21.851 --> 00:00:23.395
Or maybe someone like this?

00:00:26.273 --> 00:00:27.524
Her?

00:00:30.944 --> 00:00:33.363
I'm asked all the time,

00:00:33.363 --> 00:00:38.743
what does it feel like to be one
of the only women working in your field?

00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:42.414
How do you cope
in such a male-dominated industry

00:00:42.455 --> 00:00:44.958
as frontline war reporting?

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.253
The question continues to baffle me.

00:00:48.295 --> 00:00:51.047
Women have been doing this work
for over a century.

00:00:52.132 --> 00:00:55.010
From Martha Gellhorn
to Clare Hollingworth,

00:00:55.051 --> 00:00:58.680
from Marguerite Higgins
to Christiane Amanpour.

00:00:58.722 --> 00:01:03.018
In fact, one of the reasons I wanted
to become a foreign correspondent

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:04.394
and a war reporter

00:01:04.436 --> 00:01:07.606
was watching these women
in the field, reporting.

00:01:09.065 --> 00:01:11.318
They were professional role models,

00:01:11.318 --> 00:01:14.279
every evening at 6pm on the news

00:01:14.279 --> 00:01:16.656
when I was a little girl
in my living room,

00:01:16.656 --> 00:01:18.825
growing up in Northern Ireland.

00:01:18.825 --> 00:01:23.330
Women reporting from
all over the world on the BBC,

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:25.332
and the men were listening to them.

00:01:26.958 --> 00:01:29.294
(Laughter)

00:01:29.336 --> 00:01:33.590
(Applause)

00:01:33.757 --> 00:01:36.176
Today when I go to war zones,

00:01:36.217 --> 00:01:42.015
very often it is a majority of women
who are actually reporting there.

00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:45.560
So when I point out
that I'm no trailblazer,

00:01:45.602 --> 00:01:47.771
the next question to come is, why?

00:01:47.812 --> 00:01:51.191
Why are so many women
becoming war reporters?

00:01:51.191 --> 00:01:53.526
(Laughter)

00:01:53.526 --> 00:01:55.779
My answer to this question
is quick and easy.

00:01:56.363 --> 00:01:59.991
Because we are really, really good at it.

00:01:59.991 --> 00:02:05.747
(Applause)

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:09.125
So good, in fact,
that war reporting today,

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:11.252
the very nature of reporting,

00:02:11.294 --> 00:02:16.424
and therefore how wars are perceived
by those we report to,

00:02:16.466 --> 00:02:19.761
has been changed by women taking the lead.

00:02:19.803 --> 00:02:21.638
The types of stories that are covered,

00:02:21.638 --> 00:02:24.557
the angles that are taken
have been shaped by the fact

00:02:24.599 --> 00:02:27.394
that more and more women
are reporting them.

00:02:30.897 --> 00:02:32.565
The debate over whether or not

00:02:32.565 --> 00:02:35.068
men and women are different
in the workplace,

00:02:35.068 --> 00:02:37.862
whether we should
highlight these differences,

00:02:37.862 --> 00:02:41.116
whether it matters, has gone on for years.

00:02:41.116 --> 00:02:43.827
In war reporting,
it hasn't always been a given

00:02:43.827 --> 00:02:46.663
that we should lean into our gender lens.

00:02:47.664 --> 00:02:51.668
For years, women who have fought
to be at the front line,

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:54.045
to be given assignments in major wars,

00:02:54.087 --> 00:02:55.672
often by male editors,

00:02:55.714 --> 00:02:58.341
have felt the pressure
not to be pigeonholed

00:02:58.383 --> 00:03:02.762
into covering "women's issues"
or "softer topics."

00:03:02.804 --> 00:03:04.764
Who here hasn’t felt this way,

00:03:04.806 --> 00:03:08.309
uf you’ve ever been one of the first women
in a male-dominated field,

00:03:08.351 --> 00:03:13.189
that pressure to be one of the guys,
to not be too emotional?

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:19.612
I was first struck
by the number of female war reporters

00:03:19.612 --> 00:03:22.157
when covering the war in Syria.

00:03:22.157 --> 00:03:26.786
It was one of my first ever
major assignments for a TV news network.

00:03:26.786 --> 00:03:29.956
I was to be smuggled
across the border from Lebanon

00:03:29.998 --> 00:03:33.126
into a rebel stronghold in early 2012.

00:03:34.127 --> 00:03:36.546
The activists who were
smuggling journalists in

00:03:36.588 --> 00:03:39.424
typically took us one at a time.

00:03:39.466 --> 00:03:42.719
The journalist who preceded me
was a female correspondent

00:03:42.761 --> 00:03:46.014
for "El Pais" newspaper in Spain.

00:03:46.055 --> 00:03:48.600
Two journalists came after me together.

00:03:48.641 --> 00:03:51.811
One of them a female
correspondent for CNN,

00:03:51.853 --> 00:03:55.732
the other a female correspondent
for "The Times of London"

00:03:55.732 --> 00:03:57.275
called Marie Colvin.

00:03:58.651 --> 00:04:01.112
Colvin wouldn't make it
out of Syria alive.

00:04:01.112 --> 00:04:06.576
She was killed by the Assad forces
while reporting on their war crimes.

00:04:06.576 --> 00:04:09.287
Yes. This is dangerous work.

00:04:10.747 --> 00:04:12.832
When I moved to Beirut in 2014,

00:04:12.874 --> 00:04:15.543
the war in Syria raged on,

00:04:15.585 --> 00:04:19.798
and the Lebanese capital had become a hub
for international journalists

00:04:19.839 --> 00:04:23.384
who were living there
and covering the war across the border.

00:04:23.426 --> 00:04:26.387
I was struck by how many
of them were women.

00:04:27.472 --> 00:04:31.142
More obviously, of course,
those who were on-camera TV reporters.

00:04:31.184 --> 00:04:35.688
But a huge amount, in many cases,
the majority of print reporters as well,

00:04:35.688 --> 00:04:37.315
were women.

00:04:37.315 --> 00:04:39.609
Walk into any bar in Beirut back then

00:04:39.609 --> 00:04:42.695
frequented by international
and Lebanese journalists

00:04:42.695 --> 00:04:44.155
and you would have been faced

00:04:44.155 --> 00:04:48.034
with a small crowd of smiling,
waving female foreign correspondents

00:04:48.034 --> 00:04:51.037
catching up between
assignments and deadlines.

00:04:53.456 --> 00:04:58.336
Now while there was plenty of camaraderie
on the assignment in Syria,

00:04:58.378 --> 00:05:01.506
the war in Afghanistan in recent years
has felt different.

00:05:02.465 --> 00:05:07.262
The US press, as the war came to an end,
was less interested in that conflict

00:05:07.303 --> 00:05:10.139
beyond what it meant
for the geopolitics in the region

00:05:10.181 --> 00:05:14.310
or US foreign affairs
and national security.

00:05:14.352 --> 00:05:18.481
The few times that I did
bump into female journalists there,

00:05:18.481 --> 00:05:20.859
the few times I bumped
into any journalists there,

00:05:20.859 --> 00:05:23.653
they were almost always women.

00:05:24.779 --> 00:05:28.449
One day, about six months
before Kabul fell to the Taliban,

00:05:28.449 --> 00:05:31.828
I went out to visit a checkpoint
on the outskirts of Kabul.

00:05:31.828 --> 00:05:34.747
At the time, there was already concern

00:05:34.789 --> 00:05:38.501
about whether or not
the Afghan forces could hold off

00:05:38.543 --> 00:05:40.712
a Taliban attack on the capital.

00:05:41.629 --> 00:05:42.881
Shortly after I arrived,

00:05:42.922 --> 00:05:47.802
another crew came,
and the soldiers got very excited.

00:05:47.844 --> 00:05:50.430
This was a news team from TOLO TV,

00:05:50.471 --> 00:05:51.890
and the correspondent

00:05:51.931 --> 00:05:55.852
was one of the most famous
journalists in Afghanistan.

00:05:55.894 --> 00:05:58.187
Her name was Anisa Shaheed.

00:05:58.187 --> 00:06:00.565
The soldiers crowded around,

00:06:00.565 --> 00:06:02.817
trying to get a photograph
with her in a selfie.

00:06:04.861 --> 00:06:06.154
So did I.

00:06:06.154 --> 00:06:08.781
(Laughter)

00:06:08.781 --> 00:06:11.534
This sort of thing happened
all the time in Afghanistan.

00:06:11.534 --> 00:06:15.163
Women bumping into one another,
reporting on that war.

00:06:15.163 --> 00:06:18.499
Between us, we covered
civilian casualties,

00:06:18.541 --> 00:06:21.711
women's rights, access to education

00:06:21.753 --> 00:06:25.381
and the hopes and dreams
Afghans had for their future.

00:06:25.423 --> 00:06:29.510
Of course, we also covered
the major news of the day, the politics,

00:06:29.552 --> 00:06:34.557
the frontline fighting and the conditions
for the Afghan security forces.

00:06:34.599 --> 00:06:38.269
But we relentlessly interviewed civilians,

00:06:38.311 --> 00:06:44.192
profiling doctors and teachers
and business people, many of them women,

00:06:44.192 --> 00:06:46.569
elevating civilian voices.

00:06:49.113 --> 00:06:51.991
In the early days
of the war in Afghanistan,

00:06:51.991 --> 00:06:56.120
while vital, risky
and important work was being done,

00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:00.458
very often the kind of images
that were making it out of the conflict

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.710
looked largely like this.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:09.592
As the war came to an end
in its final years,

00:07:09.634 --> 00:07:13.596
the predominant images
were increasingly looking like this.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.732
Now, of course, Afghanistan
had evolved and changed,

00:07:24.732 --> 00:07:28.111
but so too had those
who were carrying the lens

00:07:28.111 --> 00:07:30.530
through which the world would see it.

00:07:32.198 --> 00:07:34.867
Now, none of this is to negate

00:07:34.867 --> 00:07:39.664
the vital and important work
our male colleagues do in the field.

00:07:39.706 --> 00:07:44.669
Male journalists have been
and continue to do brilliant reporting.

00:07:45.712 --> 00:07:47.964
But what I want to draw attention to

00:07:48.006 --> 00:07:51.884
is the rapid growth
of female journalists in the field

00:07:51.926 --> 00:07:55.722
and also the impact they've had
on the reporting itself

00:07:55.763 --> 00:07:58.016
that has come out of war zones
around the world.

00:07:59.767 --> 00:08:02.311
Now, when wars break out,

00:08:02.353 --> 00:08:04.939
it's humanizing images like this

00:08:04.939 --> 00:08:08.735
that are predominantly
broadcast around the world

00:08:08.735 --> 00:08:13.406
that show how families and communities
are impacted by war.

00:08:13.406 --> 00:08:15.700
They're no longer the exception.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.785
They are the norm.

00:08:18.995 --> 00:08:23.082
When Vladimir Putin
invaded Ukraine in February 2022,

00:08:23.124 --> 00:08:25.877
the images that were broadcast
around the world

00:08:25.918 --> 00:08:31.007
were of families saying goodbye to fathers
in Kyiv train station,

00:08:31.049 --> 00:08:35.595
children clutching their pets
in underground bunkers

00:08:35.636 --> 00:08:40.558
and the elderly clamoring
over broken bridges, trying to escape.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:45.354
It was these human images
that connected millions of people

00:08:45.354 --> 00:08:49.984
to what it was really like there,
for people to live through that war.

00:08:50.985 --> 00:08:53.905
Many of the stories
coming from the war in Ukraine

00:08:53.905 --> 00:08:55.406
were reported by women.

00:08:56.074 --> 00:08:57.533
Earlier this year,

00:08:57.533 --> 00:09:00.411
the Ukraine reporting team
for "The Washington Post"

00:09:00.411 --> 00:09:04.207
was awarded an award
for courage in journalism

00:09:04.248 --> 00:09:07.210
for its huge, female-led teams

00:09:07.251 --> 00:09:11.297
of editors, writers,
photographers and journalists.

00:09:12.381 --> 00:09:16.260
And who could forget the women of Iran,

00:09:16.302 --> 00:09:18.096
who not only are protesting

00:09:18.137 --> 00:09:20.932
against the repressive edicts
of their government,

00:09:20.973 --> 00:09:25.937
but leading the fight to make sure
the world covers their story.

00:09:27.396 --> 00:09:31.150
Every day, women activists
and journalists in Iran

00:09:31.150 --> 00:09:33.694
fight to make sure, and risk their lives,

00:09:33.694 --> 00:09:37.406
to make sure the images and videos
of their struggle,

00:09:37.406 --> 00:09:42.161
their protests and the crackdown
against them, make it out.

00:09:42.161 --> 00:09:45.456
It's those voices that I remember most,

00:09:45.498 --> 00:09:48.668
filling my apartment in New York City
in the middle of the night.

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:54.132
For journalists like me and many others
who cannot access the country,

00:09:54.173 --> 00:09:56.843
we have been able
to make contact with these women.

00:09:57.802 --> 00:10:01.472
They use slow internet connections
and outlawed VPNs.

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:06.727
Now, if you're going to report
from a war zone,

00:10:06.769 --> 00:10:09.355
female camaraderie does help, too.

00:10:10.982 --> 00:10:14.652
When Kabul fell to the Taliban
in August 2021,

00:10:14.652 --> 00:10:18.281
I was part of a small group
of international journalists

00:10:18.281 --> 00:10:20.533
who decided to stay at the airport

00:10:20.533 --> 00:10:24.287
and continue on their reporting
on the evacuations.

00:10:24.287 --> 00:10:28.457
Part of that group included
a female producer for British TV,

00:10:28.499 --> 00:10:31.544
as well as a female correspondent
for Danish television.

00:10:31.586 --> 00:10:33.421
Between the three of us,

00:10:33.462 --> 00:10:36.716
we shared everything
from a precious clean shirt

00:10:36.757 --> 00:10:40.344
to eyeliner and hairbrushes.

00:10:40.386 --> 00:10:42.430
We may have been reporting
from a war zone,

00:10:42.471 --> 00:10:45.308
but we all knew
the pressures of being a woman

00:10:45.349 --> 00:10:47.351
who had to be on TV that night.

00:10:48.728 --> 00:10:51.147
And I know what a lot of people wonder.

00:10:52.064 --> 00:10:54.358
What about the tough stuff?

00:10:55.318 --> 00:11:00.573
What about the sleeping in trenches,
lugging gear, coming under fire

00:11:00.573 --> 00:11:02.825
and the generally rough living conditions?

00:11:03.576 --> 00:11:05.912
I get asked all the time,

00:11:05.912 --> 00:11:08.247
how do you take a shower?

00:11:08.289 --> 00:11:10.333
My male colleagues never get asked.

00:11:11.959 --> 00:11:16.380
It seems absurd that we have to
keep answering these questions.

00:11:16.422 --> 00:11:18.549
But again and again,

00:11:18.591 --> 00:11:21.469
women have proven
that they are just as tough,

00:11:21.510 --> 00:11:23.346
brave and stoic

00:11:23.387 --> 00:11:26.599
when faced with the physical
and emotional challenges

00:11:26.641 --> 00:11:29.060
of reporting from war zones as the men.

00:11:32.063 --> 00:11:33.356
Why wouldn't we be?

00:11:33.356 --> 00:11:35.650
We've been doing it for decades,

00:11:35.650 --> 00:11:37.485
since the Spanish Civil War,

00:11:37.485 --> 00:11:40.321
World War II and the War in Vietnam,

00:11:40.321 --> 00:11:44.367
even though women at the time
were a tiny minority.

00:11:45.576 --> 00:11:51.415
Since those who came before us
nudged the door open just enough,

00:11:51.457 --> 00:11:55.419
the number of women
who have been able to in the last 20 years

00:11:55.461 --> 00:11:58.089
get assigned stories
as editors, photographers,

00:11:58.130 --> 00:11:59.757
writers and broadcasters

00:11:59.799 --> 00:12:01.509
in major war zones

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:03.511
has massively increased.

00:12:05.429 --> 00:12:07.932
We've not only increased in numbers,

00:12:07.974 --> 00:12:13.229
but crucially, we've increased
in our confidence to tell stories

00:12:13.271 --> 00:12:16.857
that harnesses our unique perspective

00:12:16.857 --> 00:12:19.860
and our own unique strengths.

00:12:20.903 --> 00:12:24.073
Now many of the things
we may have feared in the past

00:12:24.073 --> 00:12:25.741
would be held against us,

00:12:25.741 --> 00:12:29.120
our compassion, our empathy

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:32.540
and our focus on civilian lives

00:12:32.581 --> 00:12:35.251
have become our greatest strengths.

00:12:38.421 --> 00:12:41.966
We're also seeing that reflected
in our male colleagues' reporting as well.

00:12:43.134 --> 00:12:49.307
A focus on how war impacts
communities and families more broadly

00:12:49.348 --> 00:12:51.017
has become the norm.

00:12:53.185 --> 00:12:59.942
We are not just good at this job
because we are empathetic and softer

00:12:59.942 --> 00:13:03.654
and have a really good eye
for a human story.

00:13:03.654 --> 00:13:08.034
We're good at this reporting
because we're soft and empathetic

00:13:08.034 --> 00:13:11.662
and strong and tough and brave.

00:13:12.413 --> 00:13:16.167
We have extraordinary range,

00:13:16.208 --> 00:13:19.670
and it is female range that is added

00:13:19.712 --> 00:13:23.966
to the range of voices
and stories and faces

00:13:24.008 --> 00:13:26.635
that are making it out
and in front of the public

00:13:26.677 --> 00:13:28.304
from war zones today.

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:33.392
When the world is presented to you,

00:13:33.434 --> 00:13:38.564
not just in television and radio
and print and magazine,

00:13:38.564 --> 00:13:40.107
by a male gaze

00:13:40.107 --> 00:13:43.652
but by a female reporter as well,

00:13:43.652 --> 00:13:47.239
our attitudes to the outside
world change, too.

00:13:48.240 --> 00:13:49.825
We feel more connected.

00:13:50.451 --> 00:13:54.121
We can see beyond the statistics,

00:13:54.121 --> 00:13:57.541
the politics and just the war-fighting.

00:14:00.336 --> 00:14:04.173
We as journalists
are at heart communicators,

00:14:05.424 --> 00:14:11.013
and it is female reporting
that is helping the world better commune.

00:14:12.098 --> 00:14:13.349
Thank you.

00:14:13.391 --> 00:14:18.813
(Applause)

